#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083132 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384f8f04-972d-50cb-a023-dba29924242e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "f524667d-8cd2-5b91-a9e0-538fc64399e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8869" :question/problem "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90)$." :question/worked-solution "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $120$:![](images/fb/fb213f6ac09abbf31aacfdbfbd08b76dbea4199528abeb1c8041a73f0d7780ed.png)\nTherefore, $120 = 2^3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5$.\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $90$:![](images/03/034b3fe25edb2741abb035b8ca71a3c499fe6fae1a5975b4241d6379b819f19e.png)\nTherefore, $90 = 2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5$.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n120 &= 2^{3} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n90 &= 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\cdot 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $120$ and $90$ is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90) = 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5 = 360\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083133 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28dd57d-e6e5-5704-94a4-5f9bfb37f383" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Denominator of Two Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "7c928766-9971-565a-b38b-5c97b2c3ee1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8870" :question/problem "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\dfrac{4}{45}$ and $\\dfrac{7}{48}$." :question/worked-solution "The lowest common denominator of two fractions is the least common multiple of the denominators.\n\nSo, to find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $45$:![](images/5e/5e53857c25c4b7fd8dd1897dcd1b0608e48512d5a61a200a11a3f8a0ca92fcd2.png)\nTherefore, $45= 3^2 \\cdot 5$.\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $48{:}$![](images/e2/e21d5cf36f2f08fbbb46e1d923d84e0b02f10531c42c590ab7d3a813f34009dc.png)\nTherefore, $48 = 2^4 \\cdot 3$.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n45 &= 3^{2} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n48 &= 2^{4} \\cdot 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $4$\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is\n\n$$\n2^4\\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5 = 720\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746e99f3-561d-5212-ba2a-3d34b8bcdbee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Three Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "1373d2e3-fc83-56f2-b779-8f1e2d10efea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8874" :question/problem "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(30, 33, 20)$." :question/worked-solution "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30$:![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\nTherefore, $30 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5$.\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $33$. We easily deduce that $33 = 3 \\cdot 11$.\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $20{:}$![](images/7e/7e03a139fc3afa7df4d7f32ecf42f8f30d19bfe0415725f47fe5be63301899b5.png)\nTherefore, $20=2^2 \\cdot 5$.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n33 &= 3 \\cdot 11 \\\\\n20 &= 2^{2} \\cdot 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $2$\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n- the highest power of $11$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $30$, $33$, and $20$ is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(30, 33, 20) = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 11 = 660\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "384f8f04-972d-50cb-a023-dba29924242e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e5d67ca-c6be-58ec-93e1-49682dfb7816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081072} {:answer/id #uuid "dc602949-cf5e-5080-af92-438ca4278609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081073} {:answer/id #uuid "f43aeca9-0bb1-5885-900e-f0c10e1086f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081074} {:answer/id #uuid "2421020d-932b-5f28-8f95-accb6c2498b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "490" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081075} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdadefc-44a0-5572-b317-7769cf413f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081076}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a35835-75d5-5fbb-9a3c-74eb04a5a5e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081071}] :question/id #uuid "4df64354-ee14-501d-92ab-db3871c23e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-40529" :question/problem "What is $LCM(98, 175)$?"}]}
